{
  "headline": "Magnitude 4.8 earthquake strikes near Leongatha, Victoria",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Geoscience Australia detected a preliminary magnitude 4.8 earthquake at 2:01 a.m. near Leongatha, located 130km south-east of Melbourne. The quake was estimated to have occurred at a depth of approximately 10km. Tremors were felt across Melbourne's eastern suburbs and other areas including Narre Warren, Garfield, Preston, Heidelberg Heights, and Venus Bay. Geoscience Australia reported receiving thousands of accounts from individuals who felt the earthquake. The Bureau of Meteorology stated there was no tsunami threat resulting from the event. The earthquake occurred in South Gippsland, a region identified as being among the most active seismic zones in Australia. This event was the largest recorded in the state since a magnitude 5.0 earthquake on the Great Ocean Road in 2023. An aftershock with a magnitude of 3.1 was recorded 55 minutes after the initial quake. Additionally, a magnitude 3.3 earthquake was recorded on the morning of October 1, 2026, at Yarram, approximately 80km east of Leongatha."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "EOCO working with Attorney-General to arraign Manhyia South MP before weekend court",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Economic and Organised Crime Office (EOCO) stated on October 2, 2026, that it is working with the Office of the Attorney-General to bring Manhyia South MP Nana Agyei Baffour Awuah before a weekend court. According to a statement issued by EOCO, the move follows the MP's voluntary surrender to the agency at 10 a.m. on October 1, 2026. EOCO noted that the action aligns with constitutional timeframes and procedural requirements governing bail. The agency also dismissed reports claiming the MP had been granted GH¢50 million bail, urging the public to disregard such information. Investigations and other procedural processes involving the MP remain ongoing."
    },
    {
      "text": "On October 2, 2026, Minority Leader Alexander Afenyo-Markin questioned EOCO's decision to search the MP's residence. Speaking to the media after visiting Mr. Awuah in EOCO custody, Mr. Afenyo-Markin claimed the MP found the decision puzzling because he believed the court order obtained by the agency specifically authorized a search of his law firm in Dzorwulu rather than his residence. Mr. Afenyo-Markin stated that the issue arose after EOCO informed the detained MP of their intention to search his residence and noted that the court had directed the order be served on Speaker of Parliament Alban Bagbin."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Kofi Bentil describes arrest of Manhyia South MP as political witch-hunt",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Kofi Bentil, a lawyer and Senior Vice-President of IMANI Ghana, has characterized the arrest of Manhyia South MP Nana Agyei Baffour Awuah by the Economic and Organised Crime Office (EOCO) as a politically motivated witch-hunt. Speaking during a JoyNews Newsfile discussion on Saturday, October 3, Bentil, who is also a business strategy lecturer and consultant, alleged that members of both the New Patriotic Party (NPP) and the National Democratic Congress (NDC) recognize the arrest as a political attack. Bentil questioned the specific charges against the legislator, noting reports that he was deemed to have taken more than proper fees from both sides."
    },
    {
      "text": "The arrest followed a disputed attempt to take the MP into custody at the Accra High Court on September 23. EOCO stated the action was intended to secure the MP's attendance after previous invitations were not honored. However, Baffour Awuah’s lawyers challenged this account, asserting that a representative of his law firm had already engaged with investigators and provided the requested information. During the same broadcast, legal practitioner and NDC Communications Team member Theophelious Dzimegah Jnr defended EOCO, arguing the agency was performing its legally mandated duties without violating the MP's human rights and maintained that Baffour Awuah should have submitted to investigators due to his connection to the law firm involved in the transactions under investigation."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Megapoll finds nearly half of UK voters support rejoining EU",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A poll by the Messina Group (TMG) found that almost half of all voters support Prime Minister Andy Burnham’s preference to rejoin the European Union after the next election. The findings suggest that renegotiating the UK’s relationship with the EU could increase the Labour Party's vote share by as much as seven points, provided the prime minister offers a fresh referendum and campaigns for re-entry into the bloc. Regarding the decision-making process, almost a third of voters stated an in-out referendum would be required first, while more than one in six believed a general election mandate would be sufficient, and an equal proportion felt parliament could make the decision. On September 29, 2026, Burnham used his first Labour conference speech as leader to announce a review of options for Britain’s future relationship with Europe, noting that these options should include rejoining entirely."
    },
    {
      "text": "The poll results come amid internal political caution within the Labour government. Two-thirds of cabinet ministers, including Burnham, first secretary Louise Haigh, chancellor John Healey, and foreign secretary Ed Miliband, represent seats that backed Brexit and have previously stated they would respect the 2016 vote. Some Labour MPs have expressed concerns that testing public support for rejoining could pose political risks and potentially benefit Reform. Several ministers have also publicly cautioned against the move: deputy leader Lucy Powell stated going beyond inherited EU reset plans was “not possible nor desirable”; trade secretary Jonathan Reynolds cited “constitutional questions” causing business uncertainty; and home secretary Shabana Mahmood noted that rejoining is “not a simple, straightforward thing” and that costs might be unacceptable."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "John Dramani Mahama arrives in Egypt for AU summit and Alamein Africa Forum",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "President John Dramani Mahama has arrived in Alamein, Egypt, to participate in the eighth African Union Mid-Year Coordination Summit and the inaugural Alamein Africa Forum. The forum is scheduled to open on Saturday with an address by Egyptian President Abdel Fattah el-Sisi, followed by a keynote address from Nigerian businessman Aliko Dangote. The Heads of State meeting for the AU Mid-Year Coordination Summit is set to take place on September 27, 2026. These gatherings aim to bring together governments, businesses, and financial institutions to explore investment opportunities and strengthen partnerships to advance Africa’s economic integration and sustainable development."
    },
    {
      "text": "During the proceedings, President Mahama is scheduled to deliver a keynote address at a plenary session titled “Investing in Health: Manufacturing and Regional Value Chains.” His address is expected to examine healthcare as a social responsibility and a strategic economic sector with investment potential, with a focus on manufacturing and the development of regional value chains. Additionally, President Mahama is expected to hold bilateral meetings on the sidelines of the events, including talks with President el-Sisi."
    }
  ]
}
